$20?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** ${R}^{20}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3352e7cc-48c1-5ba2-8c43-5a9a559c7959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561717} {:answer/id #uuid "4518c433-5ce5-53b9-818b-7949053001bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561754} {:answer/id #uuid "8188b47d-292e-5107-a0dc-807a7df0316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561905} {:answer/id #uuid "81d0a7c9-6149-530d-b21f-31487647862e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561907} {:answer/id #uuid "e64e39b4-5532-5b56-8d2b-5ff637bec572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e64e39b4-5532-5b56-8d2b-5ff637bec572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562147} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1e5ad85-7ea2-5e0e-ae4a-daf2ef96b515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "317a01e3-d69d-5965-940a-bd52e056d6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106906" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{500}(R),$ the vector space of all $500\\times 500$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. $C[0,1],$ the vector space of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{1500}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{500}(R)$ and ${R}^{1500}$  are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{500}(R))={500}^{2}=250\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{1500})=1\\,500\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C[0,1]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $C[0,1]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $C[0,1]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d5d53b-9cf4-514c-9b8a-86262156b32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561581} {:answer/id #uuid "594364b2-4a97-5cb8-8885-eb54b077d39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561802} {:answer/id #uuid "a41a0e66-d5c5-5bf7-bd1f-361b76eac535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561990} {:answer/id #uuid "cb655fa3-bc47-5d6a-a78a-558e30d1c629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562090} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3eec37-697c-58aa-a969-a676ed98da94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594364b2-4a97-5cb8-8885-eb54b077d39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561802} :answer-field/id #uuid "05ae62a1-afd4-5a8a-9171-ff9d830fc106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae384128-da5c-5a65-8194-114971516a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106909" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{2},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R),$ the vector space of all $400\\times 400$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{2}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{2}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{200}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{400}(R))=400\\cdot 400=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1973d90e-d739-5e54-ab68-75b6c1f7b724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561653} {:answer/id #uuid "43942a52-bb00-5c25-8a66-6b236060584b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561746} {:answer/id #uuid "4a966cdf-1217-52d0-b10d-01a030f8bcf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561774} {:answer/id #uuid "58e6450d-c774-5ea8-9bf3-16d7ec9f531f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561801} {:answer/id #uuid "cefa2845-8d96-5d5c-9980-430390ea2c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cefa2845-8d96-5d5c-9980-430390ea2c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562097} :answer-field/id #uuid "2334e2ca-e972-51d4-a0dc-86a2cf81b8c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30efcbe6-dda5-5a19-8bde-8531afb9c0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36479" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{8}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{8}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a2070b-ee01-5111-a61c-4347e5d15c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483595} {:answer/id #uuid "14be6a81-848a-5ae7-b43b-71982638b4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483602} {:answer/id #uuid "71cae8a4-5a70-59fa-850b-21fa607637c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483791} {:answer/id #uuid "7427b7f3-6b05-5734-9feb-603f47022317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483800} {:answer/id #uuid "d039d520-3a75-55a1-9af7-a275fca2bb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71cae8a4-5a70-59fa-850b-21fa607637c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483791} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae82675-ac3d-59f8-b4e1-4558ec943bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d3c2164-c9a8-58f5-b6ac-e24eb41f738f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95212" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{10}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{10}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c5ae1a-9e7a-5d8b-ab09-dd7199e67922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} {:answer/id #uuid "71c1cca6-df0f-5b2c-a21a-33d4064bd36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561867} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3cea84-cf2c-5de8-8b3e-ec044d0938a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561930} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8d20b-17c6-5858-b1f2-709c110bbb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561967} {:answer/id #uuid "f454b9f0-465f-5b23-8ad6-a4f87f6b3abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44c5ae1a-9e7a-5d8b-ab09-dd7199e67922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaf5f5a-9372-5c9a-b844-dc81456ba76a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ca0b310-c54f-553d-a3d3-7e3881bc581e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95215" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R))=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645b2e7b-ed0a-5176-a522-b400791972fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561828} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d7e4db-ae01-5275-b4c8-0fd8386be685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562002} {:answer/id #uuid "b27b7ca7-3a76-56c0-845b-41bdb8aea4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562028} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83f83-0973-5661-b724-fbd23032d35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562134} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a757e4-19f6-50f1-9887-1b2bae99eff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1a757e4-19f6-50f1-9887-1b2bae99eff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69d9c07-c486-5d96-be8f-04bbd335d396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7efa12c8-5625-54e7-8464-2de1d85a3ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95218" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ the vector space of all $7\\times 7$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9255a3-f808-53ab-9a1d-bce0b2aee8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494406} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad4f74f-98bf-5d22-9457-443e51eb0e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494468} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d9093c-41de-586e-8b9a-a4e06e3dceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494702} {:answer/id #uuid "b2867c1c-1f11-5f60-8eeb-04a47f542fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494734} {:answer/id #uuid "c9096bb1-9011-5dc6-aea5-546459a5a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2867c1c-1f11-5f60-8eeb-04a47f542fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494734} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b5c8af9-d959-569a-8058-100af23b2c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "872bbc86-b2d9-5fc0-a340-666aa227cedf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95225" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $12?$\n\nI. ${R}^{12}$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 4$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}_{11}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $11$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{12})=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R))=3\\cdot 4=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{11}[t])=11+1=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32aca3cf-a5e2-53e0-9465-8c5812ddcb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494418} {:answer/id #uuid "500ce576-4c92-5f17-8883-d14fb5dd6073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494479} {:answer/id #uuid "9317dd78-6bb1-51d8-9527-c7283331155e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494653} {:answer/id #uuid "cab46aea-83c2-537f-b7c2-3ead6ff50e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494797} {:answer/id #uuid "ce013736-d830-5648-bf13-13959d355826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32aca3cf-a5e2-53e0-9465-8c5812ddcb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494418} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae053587-50f1-5f8c-b674-29dc44c5f648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "300def0f-69c7-5821-80a9-ead2c4071d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-95228" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $18?$\n\nI. ${R}_{17}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $17$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{17}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{17}[t])=17+1=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R))=6\\cdot 3=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{17})=17\\ne 18.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0d7093-d23a-56ed-9ebe-e8cae0bf1583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151764} {:answer/id #uuid "9332984e-d4f3-5c83-9c85-15c6b80b05e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151765} {:answer/id #uuid "085d9ff6-4647-5320-8166-7fd2905e1855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151766} {:answer/id #uuid "1b11fc68-6513-585b-957a-6e0ea33524a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151767} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef66779-081f-5eda-82b4-3ed4176bc616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e0d7093-d23a-56ed-9ebe-e8cae0bf1583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151764} :answer-field/id #uuid "b408dce0-ca2d-5ac0-810c-dcc878bc60db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a7f694e-9eb9-56c4-8f1f-0236761451e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95236" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{2},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R),$ the vector space of all $100\\times 1\\,000$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{2}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{2}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{2}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R))=100\\cdot 1\\,000=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfb76c40-7724-5451-8aac-19d2a8b57bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469752} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f44b9f-b85e-5529-ac9f-fe0670b28b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f44b9f-b85e-5529-ac9f-fe0670b28b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469753} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b049e3-3f6a-5ae0-9695-c15b320edad7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe85bc-6a5b-5101-83b8-3cbc7f9dadf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469754} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab4000e-252f-55bb-ae53-9aff79c28b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbe85bc-6a5b-5101-83b8-3cbc7f9dadf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469754} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4771de7-dcbb-5f3d-b4f0-14014f000a86" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "725da332-7f7a-56cd-b1c7-eb7e1e678523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469756} {:answer/id #uuid "72759b00-fedb-5a37-ab83-88c976d1613c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72759b00-fedb-5a37-ab83-88c976d1613c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469757} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7d2395-b54b-5c6b-a214-dab27c08b450" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ccb2591-5640-5714-9681-7f41d3b4bf27" :question/math-academy-id "q-95239" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n- **I.** ${R}^{200}$\n- **II.** $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- **III.** ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 200$ matrices over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R)$ and ${R}^{200}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200}(R))={200}^{2}=40\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{200})=200\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421e0432-70af-50a9-82b2-f19fe9112141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561742} {:answer/id #uuid "502d648a-8a08-58b8-9013-488b7bec9551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561788} {:answer/id #uuid "57f07402-e044-5281-893f-cc74f864e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561798} {:answer/id #uuid "688dc251-c0d0-5865-9d76-ab3397fd5d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561843} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f8436b-d8f9-5750-be2a-bffca858b1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421e0432-70af-50a9-82b2-f19fe9112141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561742} :answer-field/id #uuid "669252e0-c0d4-531b-90be-c655fcf428ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df717d4d-32a8-529f-8ad7-781f9e19e4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95387" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R)$ and ${R}_{500}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R))=200\\cdot 800=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{500}[t])=500+1=501,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of functions in ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,x,\\,{x}^{2},\\,…,{x}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{x}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [-1,1]$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ of size $n,$ which imples that ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
